This discussion brings up a very interesting point.  These are really 2 v=
ery=20
different forms of model-averaging being discussed.  Jeff is referring to=
 the=20
Burnham & Anderson (2002) approach of using information-theoretic model=20=

weights to combine models and thus account for model selection uncertaint=
y.=20=20
Len is referring to the Buckland et al. (1997) approach, which uses boots=
trap=20
methods which allow information-theoretic model selection at each bootstr=
ap=20
iteration, and is implemented in program Distance.  Both are well-justifi=
ed=20
theoretically, and as far as I know, there hasn't been much written on=20=

comparing the two approaches.=20=20

Also, I think that the approach suggested by Jeff is problematic when the=
re=20
are strata.  After constructing the model-averaged estimate and it's vari=
ance,=20
a lot of work needs done to get the individual strata abundance estimates=
=20
(and that's not straightforward for the novice Distance Sampling user).  =
Also,=20
won't the model-averaged estimate and it's variance be tricky when using=20=

different Key functions between candidate models?  And how about with=20
MCDS, where we may have different scale parameters?  Maybe I'm missing=20=

something?

Works Referenced
Buckland, S. T., K. P. Burnham, and N. H. Augustin. 1997. Model selection=
: an=20

integral part of inference. Biometrics 53:603-618.

Burnham, K. P., and D. R. Anderson. 2002. Model selection and multi-model=
=20
inference: a practical information-theoretic approach. Second Edition edi=
tion.=20

Springer, New York.

